  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nAn upper level ridging axis continues across the Plains this \nafternoon resulting in warm temperatures. Temperatures are \nforecast to warm into the upper 60s to upper 70s with a few \nspots likely hitting the lower 80s in the southwest and south \ncentral. Dry conditions across the southwest and breezy winds \naround 15 mph have resulted in near critical fire weather \nconditions. A weak front is forecast to move the the state this\nevening and tonight bringing a northwest wind shift. Overnight \nlows are forecast to drop down around the mid to upper 40s \ntonight.\n\nAn upper level low is forecast to pass across far northern\nManitoba bringing forth the aforementioned front tonight. This \nupper level low will flatten the upper level ridge with the \nfront bringing in a slightly cooler airmass across into the \nregion. Temperatures will dip down around the upper 50s to mid \n60s Sunday afternoon, which is still above normal for this time \nof year. Upper level ridging will build back over the Plains as \nan upper level low stalls out off the coast of California \namplifying the ridge early next week. This pattern will persist\nthrough much of next week. Which will lead to dry conditions \nand above normal temperatures. High temperatures are forecast to\nbe in the 60s to lower 70s through next week. \n\nBy the end of the week, the ENS and GEFS ensembles very on \nsolutions, but move the upper low off the coast of California \nthrough the Central Plains. It is to early to tell who will be \ngetting the precipitation next weekend when the pattern switches\nto more active.",
